## STILL MISSING — the one real blocker

### FINITENESS — and it is ONE gap, not two

Cycle notation and cardinality look like separate blockers; they are the same
missing piece, and the distinction matters for what to build.

- **Cycle notation is NOT blocked on sequences.** `std/sequence.b4m`'s `Value` is
  a bare abstract sort and several theories already model it at different targets,
  so a finite list of POINTS is available today. What a cycle needs beyond the
  list is its SUPPORT — {x : σ(x) ≠ x} — to be finite. "Disjoint" quantifies over
  the support and the decomposition theorem inducts on it.
- **The finiteness is not a convenience — the theorem is FALSE without it.**
  "Every permutation is a product of disjoint cycles" fails on an infinite set,
  where a permutation may move infinitely many points. The obstacle is not that
  2b4m cannot express the theorem; it is that the theorem needs a hypothesis 2b4m
  cannot state.
- **Cardinality** (`|S_n| = n!`, `|A_n| = n!/2`, `|D_n| = 2n`) needs the same
  thing plus a counting function.

PARTIAL PROGRESS (`36d087a`, then 2026-09-25/26): `std/set/finite.b4m` defines
EQUINUMEROSITY by bijection and finiteness as equinumerosity with an initial
segment, and now has the whole counting core: size UNIQUENESS (the pigeonhole
induction `segmentSizeIsUnique`, via `swap` + the restriction lemma), symmetry of
equinumerosity (bijections invert, by definite description), `hasSizeIsUnique` for
any set, and the COUNTING FUNCTION `size(a)` (`sizeIsTheSize`, `sizeOfSegment`,
`sizeOfEmptyset`). `std/permutation.b4m` adds the SUPPORT of a permutation as a set
(`supportMember`), that a permutation preserves its support, disjoint-support
permutations COMMUTE, transpositions (involution, permutation, own inverse), and
`permutes(f, a)` closed under identity/composition/inverse/transpositions.

2026-09-26 later: the FINITE-SET COMBINATORICS landed in `std/set/finite.b4m`
(with `singleton`/`subset` and the one-element-at-a-time set facts added to
`std/set.b4m`): removing an element from a set of size n+1 leaves size n, adding
a fresh one to size n gives n+1 (`removingAnElementDropsTheSize`,
`addingAnElementRaisesTheSize`), a SUBSET of a finite set is finite and no larger
(`subsetOfFiniteIsFinite`), a PROPER subset is strictly smaller
(`properSubsetIsSmaller`), and DISJOINT SIZES ADD (`disjointUnionSizesAdd`).

2026-09-26, later still: CYCLE NOTATION landed as `std/permutation/cycle.b4m` —
a `Points` list sort read by `at`, `distinct`/`avoids`, and `cycle(s, k)` =
(a₀ … a_{k-1}) DEFINED by Judson's factorization (a₀ a_{k-1})∘(a₀ … a_{k-2}); a
permutation by induction; the textbook action proved under distinctness
(`cycleFixesTheUnlisted`, `cycleWrapsAround`, `cycleAdvances`); one-cycle =
identity, two-cycle = transposition.

ORBITS landed the same day: `std/function/iterate.b4m` (f^n(x), with
f^(n+1)(x) = f^n(f(x)) and f^(m+n) = f^m ∘ f^n) and `std/permutation/orbit.b4m`
(the orbit as a comprehension set; the point is in it, it is closed under the
map, it lies inside support(f) ∪ {x}, so it is finite when the support is).

The PERIOD landed too: `std/set/finite.b4m` now has the PIGEONHOLE principle
(a map from a set of size n+1 into one of size n sends two points to the same
place), and `std/permutation/period.b4m` packages i ↦ f^i(x) as a map on the
numerals, collides it on the finite orbit, cancels the collision to a return
f^(p+1)(x) = x, and takes the least such p by well-ordering.

And the CYCLE OF AN ORBIT: `Points` has singleton/prepend postulates, the list
of iterates up to the least period exists and is distinct, and
`std/permutation/orbit-cycle.b4m`'s `orbitIsACycle` says the cycle on it agrees
with f on the orbit and fixes everything else — the one-orbit case of the
decomposition.

THE DECOMPOSITION THEOREM IS PROVED (`std/permutation/decomposition.b4m`,
`everyPermutationOfAFiniteSetIsAProductOfDisjointCycles`): peel one orbit's
cycle off, the remainder's support is a proper subset, strong induction on
`size(support)`, prepend the cycle to the remainder's sequence. So is the
TRANSPOSITION decomposition (`std/permutation/transpositions.b4m`, Judson's
Proposition 5.12): a cycle's list of transpositions is its definition read as
a prepend fold, and the disjoint cycles' lists concatenate. Both are cited from
`aata/5.1-permutation-groups.md`; its two cycle-structure placeholders are gone.
What 5.1 still assumes is exactly one thing: Judson's parity lemma.

For the counts:
a size of S_n — i.e. counting a set of FUNCTIONS, which needs sets of `Fn`
(`element.Element` is the only member sort) or a Nat-indexed enumeration of the
permutations — and a factorial.

Blocks, in chapter 5, after 2026-09-26: `|S_n| = n!`, `|A_n| = n!/2`, `|D_n| =
2n`, and the whole cube-motions section — all COUNTING. The cycle-structure
