### Well-founded / lexicographic induction

Judson's parity LEMMA ("the identity is a product of an even number of
transpositions") is a terminating-rewrite argument on a lexicographic measure —
product length, then the position of a symbol's last occurrence — and 2b4m has
ℕ-induction only. Its four rewrite identities are statements about transpositions
named by the points they move; the first, (ab)(ab) = 1, is now proved
(`std/permutation.b4m` `transpositionIsAnInvolution`), and the other three are
pointwise case analyses of the same kind, so what blocks the lemma is the
well-founded induction alone.

The lemma is therefore ASSUMED in `aata/5.1-permutation-groups.md`, exactly as
Judson states it, and disclosed by `--axioms` — the ONLY assumption the section
makes since 2026-09-26, when the parity theory was rewired from an abstract group
with an assumed "transpositions are self-inverse" axiom onto
`std/permutation/sequence.b4m`, where that fact is a theorem. Everything
downstream of the lemma is proved.

